{"timestamp": "2026-03-17T13:00:00Z", "type": "learn-method", "method": "subtractive-fix", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "sycophants-dilemma,phantom-fix,wrong-tool-args,abandoned-branch", "baseline_passed": false, "patched_passed": false, "observations": "Subtractive Fix (Method #12) DISPROVEN. Removed 114 lines (51% reduction) from optimizer SOUL.md and LESSONS.md. Results: sycophants-dilemma +1, phantom-fix -3, wrong-tool-args -2, abandoned-branch -4 = net -8. IDENTICAL regression pattern to Sprint 1 additive methods. Content volume is NOT the cause of regressions. Any content modification (add or remove) produces the same degradation pattern. Combined with null-patch test (zero-change fork = no regression), this proves the regressions are caused by WHAT is changed, not HOW MUCH. The model-level attractor hypothesis is strengthened: baseline agent files sit at a local optimum that is fragile to perturbation in any direction."}
{"timestamp": "2026-03-17T13:58:00Z", "type": "learn-method", "method": "micro-edit", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "sycophants-dilemma,phantom-fix,wrong-tool-args", "baseline_passed": false, "patched_passed": false, "observations": "Micro-Edit (Method #13) DISPROVEN. Added exactly 2 lines (1 blank + 1 content line) to optimizer SOUL.md 'How You Think' section: advisory sentence about inspecting files before deletion. Results: sycophants-dilemma 4/6 (+1), phantom-fix 3/6 (-3), wrong-tool-args 3/5 (-2) = net -4. IDENTICAL regression pattern to ALL prior experiments (Sprint 1: 5 additive, Sprint 2: 1 subtractive). Even a 2-line advisory edit triggers the same -3/-2 degradation on baseline-passing scenarios. Combined with null-patch (0 change = 0 regression) and Sprint 1 (5-20 lines = regression) and Sprint 2 (114 lines removed = regression), this PROVES there is NO perturbation threshold. ANY content modification triggers the regression effect. The phantom-fix/-3 and wrong-tool-args/-2 pattern is INVARIANT across all 8 content-modifying experiments regardless of direction (add/remove), magnitude (2 to 114 lines), tone (coercive/advisory), or content type (guard/lesson/knowledge/section). Text-based coaching via agent file modification is FUNDAMENTALLY LIMITED to replication of known-working fixes (like C39). New behavioral improvements cannot be reliably delivered through text changes to agent files."}
{"timestamp": "2026-03-17T14:06:00Z", "type": "learn-method", "method": "micro-edit", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "sycophants-dilemma,phantom-fix,wrong-tool-args", "baseline_passed": false, "patched_passed": false, "observations": "Micro-Edit (Method #13) COMPLETED RUN \u2014 corrects prior interrupted session data. Same fork (optimizer-micro-edit), same 2-line edit to SOUL.md 'How You Think' section. Results: sycophants-dilemma 3/6\u21923/6 (0), phantom-fix 6/6\u21926/6 (0), wrong-tool-args 5/5\u21925/5 (0). SAFE NEUTRAL \u2014 zero regression, zero improvement. Net delta: 0. This CONTRADICTS the interrupted session which showed -3/-2 regression. KEY FINDING: perturbation threshold EXISTS \u2014 2 lines is below it. Text-based micro-edits are SAFE (no regression risk) but this specific edit was INEFFECTIVE (didn't change sycophants-dilemma behavior). Prior 'no threshold' conclusion was based on interrupted/unreliable session data. Gym results may have run-to-run variance \u2014 interrupted sessions should be discarded."}
{"timestamp": "2026-03-17T14:10:00Z", "type": "methodology-fix", "method": "gym-name-bug", "agent": "optimizer", "scenario": "phantom-fix,wrong-tool-args", "baseline_passed": true, "patched_passed": true, "observations": "CRITICAL METHODOLOGY FIX: Discovered ALL 8 prior experiment 'regressions' were caused by changing the agent name in agent.json, NOT by text edits. Proof: (1) Null-patch fork WITH name change \u2192 3/6 phantom-fix (regression). (2) Null-patch fork WITHOUT name change \u2192 6/6 phantom-fix (no regression). (3) Micro-edit fork WITHOUT name change \u2192 6/6 phantom-fix, 5/5 wrong-tool-args (NO regression), 3/6 sycophants-dilemma (no improvement). The gym-run.sh --lab path overlays the fork onto a copy of agents/, and the agent.json name field is read by the runtime. Changing the name causes the agent to be treated as a different agent, likely loading different cached state or triggering different initialization paths. EVERY prior experiment changed the name (e.g., optimizer-fm31-fix, optimizer-subtractive, optimizer-micro-edit), causing the invariant -3/-2 pattern. FIX: never change agent.json name in lab forks. Text-based coaching is NOT fundamentally limited \u2014 the gym methodology was flawed."}
